<script lang="ts">
	import { englishExamples as examples } from '../../utils/examples.en'
	const i18n = useI18n()
</script>
<template>
	<DocsPage
		product="Meeko i18n"
		locale="en"
		title="Namespaces"
		description="Split the catalogs and load the messages each route needs."
	>
		<h2 id="configuration">common and additional catalogs</h2>
		<DocsCode
			label="meeko.config.ts"
			bind:code="examples.namespaces"
		/>
		<p>
			<code>common</code>
			is always loaded, together with the namespaces of
			<code>global</code>, such as the catalog of a shared shell.
			<code>routes</code>
			maps exact IDs of the manifest to additional namespaces. Every language declares the same namespaces.
		</p>
		<h2 id="example">A real namespace on this page</h2>
		<p>
			This route loads the
			<code>checkout</code>
			namespace through the site's
			<code>routes</code>
			option. The following result comes from that catalog:
		</p>
		<div class="translation-result">
			<h3>{i18n.t('checkout.title')}</h3>
			<p data-output="namespace">{i18n.t('checkout.summary', { total: 3 })}</p>
		</div>
		<DocsCode
			label="app/locales/es/checkout.json"
			bind:code="examples.checkout"
		/>
		<h2 id="keys">Organizing the keys</h2>
		<p>
			The name of the namespace organizes loading; it does not add a prefix to the keys. Here
			<code>checkout.title</code>
			comes from the
			<code>checkout</code>
			object of the JSON. Keys of different namespaces must be unique.
		</p>
		<p>The generated types include the keys of every namespace. Use each message on a route that loads its catalog: a key that TypeScript knows also needs to be available at runtime.</p>
	</DocsPage>
</template>
